<title>BELGIUM: Japan to raise domestic liquor tax - EU Commission.</title>
<headline>Japan to raise domestic liquor tax - EU Commission.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-02-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Japan will raise taxes on domestically produced spirits from October 1 as part of a deal to harmonise taxes with those on imported liquor, the European Commission said Tuesday.</p>
<p>Tax on so-called Shochu A spirit would be raised to 8,076 yen per kilolitre from 6,228 on October 1 and to 9,924 a year later, the Commission said.  </p>
<p>Tax on Shochu B, currently at 4,084 yen per kilolitre would be raised to 6,028 on October 1, to 7,976 a year later and to 9,924 in 2001.</p>
<p>The moves come as the European Commission and Japan finally settled a long-running row over different taxes applied by Japan to domestic and imported spirits.</p>
<p>Japanese tax on imports of brown spirits -- which include whisky and brandy -- will be slashed from 24,558 yen per kilolitre to 13,775 in October and 10,225 a year later.</p>
<p>Tax on imports of white spirits such as vodka and gin will be cut to 9,924 yen in October from 9,927, while tax on liqueurs will be raised to 9,924 from 8,217 at the same time.</p>
<p>-- David Fox, Brussels Newsroom ++ 32 2 287 6832</p>
